<main>
<p class="kicker">HFT Practice · Lesson 0039</p>
<h1>The Position-Sizing Pipeline: Relative Weights × Gross Multipliers</h1>
<div class="why"><strong>Why this matters:</strong> after the 2026-07-14 research batch,
a single day's position sizes in <code>run_strategy_walkforward</code> flow through up
to FOUR stages. Understanding why they compose the way they do — and why every stage
is capped at 1 — is the difference between extending this pipeline safely and
accidentally building leverage into a long-only paper trader.</div>

<h2 id="s1">1 · The pipeline, in order</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Stage</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Range</th><th>Code</th></tr>
<tr><td>1. Relative weights</td><td>How do today's picks split whatever we deploy? (confidence ÷ σ, capped per-name at max_pos, cash remainder)</td><td>Σ ≤ 1</td><td><code>_conf_vol_weights</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Vol-target multiplier</td><td>Is the LEDGER's recent volatility above target? Scale the whole book down.</td><td>(0, 1]</td><td><code>min(1, target_vol/realized_vol)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Kelly multiplier</td><td>Does this strategy's own trade HISTORY justify full deployment?</td><td>[0, 1]</td><td><code>kelly_fraction</code> — λ·μ/σ², clamped</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. ATR stop overlay</td><td>Did any held name breach its volatility-sized trail? (Removes candidates, not weights)</td><td>—</td><td>suppression + cooldown</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Stages 2 and 3 multiply: <code>final = relative × vol_mult × kelly_mult</code>. Since
each factor is ≤ 1, the product is ≤ 1 — the book can only ever de-lever. That's a
STRUCTURAL guarantee, not a hope: no configuration of flags can borrow.</p>

<h2 id="s2">2 · Why multiply, not average or min?</h2>
<p>The multipliers answer <em>independent</em> questions: realized-vol says "the market
is currently wilder than my target"; Kelly says "my measured edge is thinner than full
deployment assumes." If both are true, both discounts should apply — a 0.5 vol scale on
a 0.5 Kelly conviction means 25% deployed, and that's correct, not double-counting.
<code>min()</code> would ignore one true fact; averaging would invent a number neither
control asked for. Multiplication is the only composition that respects both.</p>

<h2 id="s3">3 · Determinism: the constraint that shaped everything</h2>
<p>Every stage feeds on data the replay can reconstruct: relative weights from price
history, the vol multiplier from the ledger's own accumulating returns, Kelly from the
run's own accumulating blotter (deliberately NOT the stored trades table — one code
path for backtest and live backfill), ATR trails from OHLC. The arena invariant —
<em>live is a deterministic recompute</em> — survives because no sizing stage reads
anything a replay can't rebuild. This is also why Kelly sizes today's bet only from
trades BEFORE today: letting today's outcome size today's bet is lookahead wearing a
risk-management costume.</p>

<h2 id="s4">4 · Often confused</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Pair</th><th>Distinction</th></tr>
<tr><td>Inverse-vol weights (stage 1) vs vol targeting (stage 2)</td><td>Stage 1 equalizes risk ACROSS names (calm names get more of the pie); stage 2 sizes the pie itself against a target. You can be perfectly risk-balanced across names and still have twice the portfolio vol you want.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kelly multiplier vs the divergence alert</td><td>Both read realized performance; Kelly quietly scales exposure with the measured edge, divergence loudly flags statistical inconsistency with the backtest. One acts on size, the other on your attention.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ATR stop vs the drawdown breaker</td><td>ATR: per-POSITION, price-path-based, auto-recovers after cooldown. Breaker: per-LEDGER (or portfolio), equity-based, human-resume only.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full Kelly vs fractional Kelly</td><td>f* = μ/σ² is optimal only if μ and σ are KNOWN. They're estimated — and overbetting a noisy edge estimate is ruinous asymmetrically. λ ∈ [0.25, 0.5] is deliberately the only offered range.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="s5">5 · Practice</h2>
<p>Score: <span id="score">0 / 0</span></p>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q1.</strong> vol_mult = 0.6 and kelly_mult = 0.5 on the same day. Deployed fraction of the book?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="min() would ignore the other control's true finding.">0.5 — the stricter control wins</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="0.6 × 0.5 = 0.30. Independent discounts compose multiplicatively; both facts get respected.">0.30</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Averaging invents a number neither control computed.">0.55</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="They don't cancel — both point the same direction: smaller.">1.0 — they offset</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q2.</strong> Why does the Kelly estimator use only trades from BEFORE today?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Speed is irrelevant — it's one mean and variance either way.">Including today would be too slow</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Sizing today's bet with today's outcome is lookahead — the same sin as trading on tomorrow's close, hidden inside a risk control.">Today's outcome sizing today's bet is lookahead</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The window is trailing anyway; the issue is WHICH trades, not how many.">The window would overflow</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Backtest and live use the SAME in-window accumulation — that's the determinism design, not a limitation.">Live can't see today's trades</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q3.</strong> A strategy's Kelly multiplier reads 0.0 in the report. What happened, and what does the arena do?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Negative estimated edge clamps to zero: the strategy keeps ticking (rows with zero exposure) — a loud, visible anomaly rather than a silent skip, and the human decides what to do.">Its measured edge went negative; it still ticks but deploys nothing</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Pausing is the breaker's job; Kelly only sizes.">The breaker paused it</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Below min_trades the multiplier is exactly 1.0, not 0.">It has too few trades</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="λ is bounded [0.25, 0.5] and scales f*, it can't be 0 itself.">The lambda flag was set to zero</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q4.</strong> The ATR stop breaches on Tuesday's close. When does the position actually stop earning returns?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Tuesday's return IS realized — no intrabar fill fantasy; the engine can't exit at a price inside a bar it only sees at close.">Tuesday — the breach bar's return is cancelled</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right: the breach bar's return stays (honest — you couldn't have known intraday), and suppression removes the ticker from Wednesday's candidates for cooldown_days.">Wednesday — via suppression from the next bar's candidates</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Cooldown controls RE-entry length, not exit timing.">After cooldown_days</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The trail is per-position; the ledger-level pause is the breaker's separate mechanism.">When the ledger's breaker also trips</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<script>
function shuffle(arr){
  for (let i = arr.length - 1; i > 0; i--){
    const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
    [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
  }
  return arr;
}
let answered = 0, right = 0;
document.querySelectorAll('[data-question]').forEach(q => {
  const list = q.querySelector('.options');
  shuffle([...list.children]).forEach(li => list.appendChild(li));
  const feedback = q.querySelector('.feedback');
  let done = false;
  list.querySelectorAll('li').forEach(li => {
    li.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const correct = li.dataset.correct === 'true';
      if (!done){ answered++; if (correct) right++; done = true; }
      li.classList.add(correct ? 'picked-right' : 'picked-wrong');
      feedback.hidden = false;
      feedback.textContent = (correct ? '✓ Correct. ' : '✗ Not quite. ') + li.dataset.why;
      feedback.style.color = correct ? 'var(--good)' : 'var(--bad)';
      document.getElementById('score').textContent = right + ' / ' + answered;
    });
  });
});
</script>
